From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Subject: [PATCH] namespace: honour ProtectHome=yes and ProtectSystem=yes from Linux 3.15

0003-Disable-ProtectHome-and-ProtectSystem-for-old-kernel.patch (2017) made
the boolean "yes" of ProtectHome= and ProtectSystem= parse as "no" on every
machine, for the old Halium kernels. Since then a mount namespace on the 3.4
and 3.10 kernels is handled by the MS_MOVE fallback in mount_switch_root_full()
(0009), but the "yes" values stayed off everywhere, including mainline 6.x/7.x
and the 4.x/5.x Halium kernels, which silently voided that hardening for every
unit using it, and systemd-analyze security reported the units as unprotected.

The package is shared by machines with very different kernels (the
aarch64-halium feed carries both bullhead-halium on 3.10 and q25 on 5.10), so
the decision cannot be made at build time. Make it at parse time instead: the
boolean "yes" still maps to "no" while the running kernel is older than 3.15,
the same cut-off 0009 uses, and to "yes" on anything newer. The explicit values
(read-only, tmpfs, full, strict) are never changed, as before.

On a kernel older than 3.15 the unit file still says "yes" and
systemd-analyze security, run offline on a newer host, shows it as set.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [systemd's minimum kernel is much newer than 3.15]
---
--- a/src/core/namespace.c
+++ b/src/core/namespace.c
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <sys/file.h>
 #include <sys/mount.h>
+#include <sys/utsname.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 #if WANT_LINUX_FS_H
 #include <linux/fs.h>
@@ -3321,7 +3322,21 @@ static const char *const protect_home_table[_PROTECT_HOME_MAX] = {
         [PROTECT_HOME_TMPFS]     = "tmpfs",
 };
 
-DEFINE_STRING_TABLE_LOOKUP_WITH_BOOLEAN(protect_home, ProtectHome, PROTECT_HOME_YES);
+/* LuneOS: on the Halium machines with 3.4 and 3.10 kernels, a mount namespace used to take the unit
+ * (or, on 3.4, the kernel) down, so ProtectHome=yes and ProtectSystem=yes were turned into "no" there.
+ * Keep doing that only while the running kernel is older than 3.15, the same cut-off as the MS_MOVE
+ * fallback in mount_switch_root_full(); newer kernels get the setting as written. The explicit
+ * values (read-only, tmpfs, full, strict) are never changed. */
+static bool protect_boolean_unsupported(void) {
+        struct utsname u;
+        unsigned major, minor;
+
+        return uname(&u) >= 0 && sscanf(u.release, "%u.%u", &major, &minor) == 2 &&
+               (major < 3 || (major == 3 && minor < 15));
+}
+
+DEFINE_STRING_TABLE_LOOKUP_WITH_BOOLEAN(protect_home, ProtectHome,
+                                        protect_boolean_unsupported() ? PROTECT_HOME_NO : PROTECT_HOME_YES);
 
 static const char *const protect_system_table[_PROTECT_SYSTEM_MAX] = {
         [PROTECT_SYSTEM_NO]     = "no",
@@ -3330,7 +3345,8 @@ static const char *const protect_system_table[_PROTECT_SYSTEM_MAX] = {
         [PROTECT_SYSTEM_STRICT] = "strict",
 };
 
-DEFINE_STRING_TABLE_LOOKUP_WITH_BOOLEAN(protect_system, ProtectSystem, PROTECT_SYSTEM_YES);
+DEFINE_STRING_TABLE_LOOKUP_WITH_BOOLEAN(protect_system, ProtectSystem,
+                                        protect_boolean_unsupported() ? PROTECT_SYSTEM_NO : PROTECT_SYSTEM_YES);
 
 static const char *const protect_control_groups_table[_PROTECT_CONTROL_GROUPS_MAX] = {
         [PROTECT_CONTROL_GROUPS_NO]      = "no",
